<BaseLayout title="How to install an MCP server safely — FreeMCP" description="Learn how to choose, configure, and test an MCP server in your AI client.">
<article class="container info-page"><div class="eyebrow">FREEMCP / LEARN</div><h1>How to install an MCP server safely</h1><p class="intro">Learn how to choose, configure, and test an MCP server in your AI client.</p><div class="info-content"><h2>1. Choose a compatible server</h2><p>Find a server in the <a href="/servers/">FreeMCP directory</a> and open its original repository. Confirm it supports your AI client and operating system.</p><h2>2. Read the maintainer's instructions</h2><p>Check prerequisites, installation commands, supported transport (such as stdio or Streamable HTTP), environment variables, and required permissions. Do not assume example commands work for every project.</p><h2>3. Inspect security requirements</h2><p>Review source code and dependencies where possible. Prefer least-privilege credentials, scoped tokens, and a test environment. Never paste secrets into public issues or chat logs.</p><h2>4. Add the server to your MCP client</h2><p>Use your client's documented configuration interface. A local server generally requires an executable command and arguments; a remote server may require an endpoint and authentication. Configuration formats differ by client.</p><h2>5. Restart and verify</h2><p>Restart or reconnect your AI client if required, confirm the server appears, and test a low-risk action. Check logs and the maintainer's troubleshooting guidance if it fails.</p><h2>6. Maintain your setup</h2><p>Keep dependencies updated, review newly requested permissions, and revoke credentials when a server is no longer needed.</p><p>For exact configuration examples, consult your <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io">client's official MCP documentation</a> and the server's README.</p></div></article>
</BaseLayout>
